  "summary": "Traditional Japanese glassware featuring intricate cut patterns created on the surface of transparent or colored glass.",
  "description": "Characterized by precise geometric and floral patterns cut into the glass surface, often with contrasting colored bases. Developed in the Edo period as a response to Western glass-making techniques, becoming highly refined during the Meiji era when it was exported internationally.",
  "culturalContext": "Edo kiriko was first produced in 1834 by Kagaya Kyūbei, a glassware merchant in Edo. In 1873, a Western-style glassworks was established in Shinagawa, Tokyo, and in 1881 British glass expert Emmanuel Hauptmann was invited to Japan to introduce advanced European techniques. These developments led to the refinement of Edo kiriko, and its pieces were subsequently showcased at international expositions, earning global recognition. As the techniques for cutting and polishing developed, the quality of Edo kiriko improved steadily, reaching the height of its popularity in the early years of the 20th century.",
